# An empty String owns no buffer, whichever of the three producers made it: a
# bare binding, a conversion of an empty view (`"".string`), or a copy of one.
# Length and capacity are what a reader can see of that, so they are what this
# pins -- and a non-empty copy still owns its own bytes.
main: function is {
    bare: String
    lit: "".string
    dup: lit.copy
    print "bare \{bare.length} \{bare.capacity}"
    print "lit \{lit.length} \{lit.capacity}"
    print "dup \{dup.length} \{dup.capacity}"
    src: "abc".string
    cp: src.copy
    print "cp \{cp} \{cp.length} \{cp.capacity}"
}
